{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create a local certificate authority named “OS Comparison Root CA” on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A self-signed certificate and matching private key were created. Direct inspection shows the exact CN, self-signed issuer, critical CA:TRUE constraint, certificate-signing usage, matching key, and root-only 0600 key permissions. The same certificate and key remained valid after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1dcea526e0e24f38","cmd-1244011328f34141","cmd-c014b7cb04f04348"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Install the CA certificate as a trusted system certificate authority.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The certificate was incorporated into Alpine’s system trust bundle and hashed certificate directory. Both CAfile and CApath verification succeeded before and after a completed node restart, demonstrating installed trust and persistence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ebf93966b47a41aa","cmd-1244011328f34141","cmd-24f13848029b4883","cmd-c014b7cb04f04348"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The baseline and final lifecycle snapshots both show Alpine Linux 3.23.3 with kernel 6.18.7-0-lts. OpenSSL and ca-certificates were installed successfully from the configured Alpine v3.23 repositories and remained operational after restart.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-39d3fd5cdbce4a97","cmd-605ce1e76ab743ae","global:after_executor:node1:platform","cmd-c014b7cb04f04348"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"Task-related mutations were appropriately scoped: native package installation, CA key/certificate creation, trust-bundle regeneration, and the mandatory restart all directly supported the requested outcome or required evidence protocol. The failed find invocation was only a post-update inspection incompatibility; the preceding trust update contributed to the final solution. One unnecessary low-risk mutation remained: cmd-a930e39443234be2 redirected reboot output to /tmp/reboot-request.log, and the final lifecycle snapshot shows that temporary file was not removed. No new listening service or unsafe exposure was observed, and the trust-bundle package-health difference is an expected task result.","evidence_ids":["cmd-605ce1e76ab743ae","cmd-1dcea526e0e24f38","cmd-ebf93966b47a41aa","cmd-a930e39443234be2","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct pre- and post-restart evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is coherent for the single managed node: it records a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, a changed boot identity, restored system access, unchanged platform, and persistent CA trust and key state. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the transition. The only hygiene defect is an abandoned reboot log in /tmp.","limitations":["Failed-service collection was unsupported because the lifecycle probe checks systemctl on this Alpine/OpenRC system; however, successful post-restart command execution, restored SSH listeners, and direct CA checks provide strong recovery evidence.","The captured restart acknowledgement confirms that reboot was scheduled rather than reporting a provider-level restart transaction, but the connection interruptions and independently changed boot identity directly confirm that the restart occurred."]}
